  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potential for moderate to heavy rainfall and a  \nfew thunderstorms today and tonight with gusty winds...\ntransitioning to widespread rain and drizzle for Wednesday \ninto Thursday.\n\nFrontal boundary has worked its way back north this afternoon,\ncurrently situated from southeast West Virginia, then east \nacross Roanoke and into northern North Carolina. Marginal\nsurface heating has occurred in this general area and as a\nresult, have seen some destablization. With the front nearby,\nthis should aid in supporting a few thunderstorms this afternoon\nalong the periphery of the front. Severe storms not expected,\nbut given the pulse-like nature of the convection, a few brief\nstronger cells cannot be ruled out. \n\nOvernight, the frontal boundary will again move southward with a\nstrong northeast push of cool maritime air in response to a\ndeveloping surface low over the Atlantic. Increasing isentropic\nlift should result in rain showers developing overnight and \ninto Wednesday as the gradient strengthens between high \npressure over southern Canada and low pressure in the Atlantic.\nCool and damp conditions will continue into Thursday, slowly \nimproving into Friday as flow becomes more northwesterly, then \nbecoming dry and mild for the weekend.\n\nHave continued to lower temperatures a few degrees below\nguidance for Weds/Thu due to the persistent cold air damming\nsignal. Highs will be below average in the 50s and 60s both\ndays..falling into the low 50s to upper 40s overnight.",
